<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  AgileX Scout V2 with a scanning laser, for driving in timber.

  The stock Scout description carries no sensors whatsoever - it is a drive
  base with four wheels and a deck. That is fine on an empty playpen and
  useless here: this robot has to cross a stand of trees between the fire
  ground and the dam, and the trees are not in any map it has.

  Where the scanner goes
  ......................
  On a mast above the deck, at 0.50 m. The Scout's body is 0.925 x 0.38 m and
  stands 0.34 m off the ground, so anything mounted at deck height looks down
  the length of its own vehicle and a reactive filter fed those returns
  believes it is boxed in. The mast clears the body, which also means a clean
  270 degrees rather than the 200 a front boom can manage without seeing
  itself.

  This is the opposite of the choice the quadrotors make about mast height,
  and for the opposite reason: an aircraft pitches to translate, so its scan
  plane tips and a short mast ends up looking through its own rotors. A Scout
  stays level, so height costs nothing here.

  The cost that does exist is that one Scout's plane passes over another one -
  their decks are at 0.34 m. That is what the visibility pole below is for,
  and with move_base planning on this scan it is not optional.

  What it still cannot see
  ........................
  The drones. They cruise at 2.0 m and come down to 1.25 m over a tender; the
  plane is at 0.50 m. That is the right way round - a ground robot that swerved
  for an aircraft a metre and a half above it would be a nuisance - but it
  means deconfliction between the fleets is vertical separation and nothing
  else, which is only sound because the one place a drone comes low near a
  Scout is the refill hover, directly over a tender that is stopped.

  It also cannot see the fires, which have no collision geometry at all. That
  is deliberate in the fire model and convenient here: a tender is allowed to
  stand close to a fire without its costmap fighting the standing point.

  Deck friction
  .............
  Carried over from scout_gazebo's own simulation overlay. base_link has no
  surface properties in the stock description, which leaves the deck at
  Gazebo's defaults - it matters here because a tender is a thing drones hover
  over and station-keep against, not just a drive base.
-->
<robot name="scout_v2" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <xacro:arg name="robot_namespace" default="/" />

  <xacro:include filename="$(find scout_description)/urdf/scout_v2.xacro" />

  <gazebo reference="base_link">
    <mu1>1.5</mu1>
    <mu2>1.5</mu2>
    <kp>1000000.0</kp>
    <kd>10.0</kd>
    <minDepth>0.001</minDepth>
    <maxContacts>32</maxContacts>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- How this robot drives, and why it is not the stock stack
       .......................................................
       scout_gazebo drives the Scout through ros_control: one
       velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController per wheel, plus its
       scout_skid_steer.py node to turn /cmd_vel into four wheel commands with
       the right signs. That is the physically honest way to do it and it is
       not available here - `velocity_controllers` is not installed on this
       machine, so all four controllers fail to load with

           Could not load controller 'scout_motor_fl_controller' because
           controller type 'velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController'
           does not exist

       and the vehicle sits there. `sudo apt install ros-noetic-velocity-
       controllers` fixes that if you would rather have the wheel-level drive;
       see the README.

       The wheel-level Gazebo plugin, libgazebo_ros_skid_steer_drive.so, is
       installed but cannot be used either, and the reason is in the Scout's
       own description: the right-hand wheels are mounted rpy="3.14 0 0",
       which flips their declared axis "0 -1 0" to "0 +1 0" in the base_link
       frame. That plugin applies one sign convention to all four joints, so a
       forward command drives the left wheels forward and the right wheels
       backward, and the robot pirouettes. Fixing it would mean redefining the
       wheel macros, which is a lot of surgery on somebody else's robot to
       avoid one apt package.

       So the tender drives with libgazebo_ros_planar_move.so, which AgileX's
       own description offers as the alternative (it is the second commented
       out block in scout_v2.gazebo). It takes /cmd_vel in the body frame and
       moves the base directly. The wheels do not turn, which is the honest
       cost of it, and for this scenario the trade is a good one: what the rest
       of the package needs from a tender is that it be *where the water board
       says it is*, because the drones fly at that position and the ledger
       decides whether a refill is happening from the same number. A kinematic
       drive is better at that than a skid-steer one, not worse. -->
  <gazebo>
    <plugin name="tender_drive" filename="libgazebo_ros_planar_move.so">
      <robotNamespace>$(arg robot_namespace)</robotNamespace>
      <commandTopic>cmd_vel</commandTopic>
      <odometryTopic>odom</odometryTopic>
      <odometryFrame>$(arg robot_namespace)/odom</odometryFrame>
      <odometryRate>50.0</odometryRate>
      <!-- Prefixed, or the TF tree is two disconnected halves: this plugin
           publishes <ns>/odom -> base_link while robot_state_publisher (with
           tf_prefix) publishes <ns>/base_link -> <ns>/laser0_frame. Nothing
           noticed while the tender drove itself by raw odometry; move_base
           notices immediately, because it has to transform a scan in
           <ns>/laser0_frame into a costmap in map. -->
      <robotBaseFrame>$(arg robot_namespace)/base_link</robotBaseFrame>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- The base is driven kinematically, so the wheels are along for the ride
       and their stock friction only fights it. Dropped to a slither, which
       keeps the vehicle from juddering as the contacts load and unload. It
       still rests on them, so it sits at the right height and the laser boom
       is where the geometry above says it is. -->
  <xacro:macro name="slick_wheel" params="name">
    <gazebo reference="${name}">
      <mu1>0.05</mu1>
      <mu2>0.05</mu2>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>
  <xacro:slick_wheel name="front_left_wheel_link" />
  <xacro:slick_wheel name="front_right_wheel_link" />
  <xacro:slick_wheel name="rear_left_wheel_link" />
  <xacro:slick_wheel name="rear_right_wheel_link" />

  <!-- The mast. Cosmetic - it gives the scanner something to stand on - but a
       real link with a real (tiny) mass, so the inertia the solver sees is the
       one the model declares. -->
  <link name="laser_mast">
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.01" />
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" />
      <inertia ixx="1e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="1e-6" iyz="0" izz="1e-6" />
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry>
        <cylinder radius="0.020" length="0.26" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="mast_grey">
        <color rgba="0.3 0.3 0.32 1.0" />
      </material>
    </visual>
  </link>

  <joint name="laser_mast_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child  link="laser_mast" />
    <!-- base_link is 0.235 m off the ground and the body top is at 0.34, so
         z = +0.135 stands the mast from the deck up to the scanner at 0.50 m. -->
    <origin xyz="0.0 0 0.135" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </joint>

  <!-- The visibility pole, and why a navigation stack needs one
       .........................................................
       A Scout's deck is 0.34 m off the ground and the scanner now sits at
       0.50 m, so one Scout's scan plane passes clean over another one. That
       was tolerable when the tender drove by a reactive filter and its
       standing points were kept metres apart by agreement; it is not
       tolerable with move_base, whose local costmap is built from this scan
       and which will happily plan a path through a vehicle it cannot see.

       A real survey vehicle solves this with a whip or a flag. So does this
       one: a 0.05 m pole from the deck to 0.75 m, with collision, mounted at
       the back of the deck. It rises 0.25 m through every other Scout's scan
       plane, which is what makes each robot a dynamic obstacle in the others'
       costmap rather than a hole in it.

       At the back rather than the middle because a drone hovers over the deck
       to take water. The pole tops out at 0.75 m and the refill hover is at
       1.25 m, so there is half a metre of clearance and 0.30 m of horizontal
       offset on top of it. -->
  <link name="visibility_pole">
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.02" />
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" />
      <inertia ixx="1e-5" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="1e-5" iyz="0" izz="1e-5" />
    </inertial>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry><cylinder radius="0.05" length="0.40" /></geometry>
    </collision>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry><cylinder radius="0.05" length="0.40" /></geometry>
      <material name="pole_orange">
        <color rgba="0.95 0.45 0.05 1.0" />
      </material>
    </visual>
  </link>

  <joint name="visibility_pole_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child  link="visibility_pole" />
    <!-- Centre at 0.55 m above ground, so the pole spans 0.35 to 0.75 m. -->
    <origin xyz="-0.30 0 0.315" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </joint>

  <!-- hector's Hokuyo, the same scanner the quadrotors carry, but opened out
       to a full 270 degrees and 361 rays. The old 200-degree boom scan was cut
       to that width purely to keep this vehicle's own chassis out of view, and
       it was enough for a three-sector reactive filter. A costmap is a
       different customer: it wants everything around the robot, because what
       it is building is a picture rather than an answer to "is anything in
       front of me". On the mast there is no self-hit to avoid, so the width
       costs nothing.

       10 Hz, as on the drones - five scanning vehicles at 40 Hz is real time
       the simulation would rather spend on physics. -->
  <xacro:include filename="$(find hector_sensors_description)/urdf/hokuyo_utm30lx.urdf.xacro" />
  <xacro:hokuyo_utm30lx name="laser0" parent="base_link" ros_topic="scan"
                        update_rate="10" ray_count="361"
                        min_angle="-135" max_angle="135">
    <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.265" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </xacro:hokuyo_utm30lx>

</robot>
